Thank you for your advices.
For the date, we consider mean date of the visit as site covariate. It
allows us to model reproduction as a function of date.
We first want to use multistate models but we can’t implement them in
unmarked. PRESENCE does it but there is less options for the
complementary analyses.
Our data don’t allow us to estimate daily survival rate. We use
behavior cues (food transportation principally) to assess reproductive
status of observed adult birds.

On 17 mai, 10:23, Dan Linden <danlin...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Aude,
>
> The problem with your current model is that date is not a site-specific
> attribute, while psi is trying to estimate a site-specific probability (if
> there are covariates).  You could model detection as varying with date
> (p~date) if you think the detectability of reproduction changes with date.
> But I'm not sure how date would affect the probability of reproduction at a
> site.
>
> I would consider using a multi-state model.  It sounds like you have data
> for sites where the species was not observed.  So you should be able to
> model the probability of presence and the probability of reproduction,
> conditional on presence (sensu Nichols et al. 2007 Ecology).  Date could
> then potentially affect your probability of detecting reproduction (delta
> in the Nichols paper).  If you are content with simply modeling the
> probability of reproduction given observed presence (using only those sites
> with observed presence), then at a least an occupancy model will allow an
> estimate of detection probability.  But you've conditioned your inference
> on sites where the species was observed, and obviously observed presence
> does not always equal presence.
>
> Like Marc said, if you are interested in estimating nest success then you
> should consider looking into models of daily survival rate (Dinsmore et al.
> 2002 and Stanley 2004).  Those models typically require more frequent
> visitations than occupancy models, if you want your survival estimates to
> have any precision.
>
> -Dan
